## Solution Approach 2: Better (Binary Search)

### Algorithm

1. Sort the array.
2. Fix the first two sides using two loops.
3. For each pair `(i, j)`, use binary search to find the first index where:
   ```
   nums[k] >= nums[i] + nums[j]
   ```
4. All elements before that index (after `j`) form valid triangles.
5. Add the count of valid third sides.
6. Return the total count.

### Code

```java
class Solution {
    public int triangleNumber(int[] nums) {

        Arrays.sort(nums);

        int n = nums.length;
        int count = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n - 2; i++) {

            if (nums[i] == 0)
                continue;

            for (int j = i + 1; j < n - 1; j++) {

                int left = j + 1;
                int right = n - 1;
                int ans = n;

                while (left <= right) {

                    int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

                    if (nums[mid] >= nums[i] + nums[j]) {
                        ans = mid;
                        right = mid - 1;
                    } else {
                        left = mid + 1;
                    }
                }

                count += ans - j - 1;
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```

### Complexity

- **Time:** `O(n² log n)`
- **Space:** `O(1)`

